Subject: [PATCH] Replace the blocking sleeps in hostBringUp with busy waits

The 2026-11-21 03:52 device run reached 'NativeUfs: host wake-settle-begin'
with the footer on ufssetl, and never printed 'host wake-settle-done'. The
wake command itself had already succeeded (cmd-return end=0 ocs=0 polls=1,
rsp-ok len=0, scsi-done status=0, wake-done printed), so the halt is inside
IOSleep (50).

hostBringUp drives the UFS command path with IODelay busy waits while
interrupts are masked (see RootCommands.h). IOSleep needs the scheduler to
wake the thread, which cannot happen in that state, so the thread never
returns. Replace both sleeps in this function with bounded IODelay busy
waits of the same duration: IOSleep (50) after the wake command and
IOSleep (10) between TEST UNIT READY retries.

Kept separate from 0004, which stays trace-only so its equivalence proof
against the post-0002 body remains valid. Apply after 0004.
---
diff --git a/NativeUfs.cpp b/NativeUfs.cpp
index 9ea32d3..f283ff6 100644
--- a/NativeUfs.cpp
+++ b/NativeUfs.cpp
@@ -1317,7 +1317,11 @@ static bool hostBringUp (const char **why)
     printf ("NativeUfs: host wake-done\n");
     printf ("NativeUfs: host wake-settle-begin\n");
     ufsCheckpoint ("ufssetl");
-    IOSleep (50);
+    // The UFS command path polls with IODelay while interrupts are masked, so a
+    // scheduler sleep can never wake this thread: the 03:52 device run stopped
+    // inside IOSleep(50), after 'host wake-settle-begin' and before the matching
+    // '-done'. Busy-wait the same 50 ms instead.
+    for (unsigned settle = 0; settle < 50; settle++) IODelay (1000);
     printf ("NativeUfs: host wake-settle-done\n");
     ufsCheckpoint ("ufssetd");
   }
@@ -1333,7 +1337,8 @@ static bool hostBringUp (const char **why)
     }
     if (gHost.poisoned) break;
     printf ("NativeUfs: host tur-retry attempt=%u\n", attempt);
-    IOSleep (10);
+    // Same reason as above: pace the retry with a busy wait, not a sleep.
+    for (unsigned pace = 0; pace < 10; pace++) IODelay (1000);
   }
   gHost.ready = false;
   *why = "LUN 0 not ready";
